<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jejakabhukti</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jejakabhukti was the medieval name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a> region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandela-dynasty">Chandela dynasty</a> from the 9th to the 13th century. Chandela inscriptions derive the name from Jeja, or Jayashakti, an early king of the line. The region covered the uplands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a>, with the forts of Kalinjar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahoba">Mahoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajaigarh">Ajaigarh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jejakabhukti.jpg" alt="Jejakabhukti" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omkarshinde19 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kandariya_Mahadev_temple_and_Devi_Jagadambi_temple,_Khajuraho_Temple_complex,_UNESCO_WHS,_Madya_Pradesh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
